ZTEXT00100001101cb5883650000000
Zero OS - shared UEFI console handoff

loader.zero serves AMD64 UEFI; loader32.zero serves IA32 UEFI. Both obtain
Graphics Output Protocol while boot services are available, copy validated
metadata, finish all mode selection/QueryMode buffer releases before the
final memory-map/exit loop, and enter the same kernel source compiled for
their architecture. The kernel renders its own glyphs after firmware services
end. It does not call GOP methods or firmware console routines after entry.

Shared handoff fields (all u64, byte offsets)
0       memory-map byte size
8       memory-descriptor stride
16      memory-map pointer
24      allocated kernel-code pages
32      memory-map key
40      memory-descriptor version
48      display kind: 0 serial only, 1 VGA text, 2 GOP framebuffer
56      framebuffer address
64      visible pixel width
72      visible pixel height
80      pixels per scan line
88      pixel format: 0 RGB-reserved, 1 BGR-reserved; four bytes per pixel
96      boot kind: 1 BIOS, 2 UEFI
104     execution architecture: 32 or 64
112     owned kernel stack-pool base
120     stack-pool size in bytes
128     native compiler bundle base
136     native compiler bundle bytes (1048576)
144     native compiler workspace base
152     native compiler workspace bytes (8388608)

Both UEFI loaders clear the complete 256-byte header. This preserves zero
upper halves when IA32 firmware writes a 32-bit UINTN result, or either
firmware writes the 32-bit descriptor-version field. BIOS supplies the same
display/boot/architecture layout. UEFI always writes boot kind 2; each loader
writes its own architecture. IA32 ABI differences are documented in UEFI32.ztext.
Each UEFI loader allocates 256 pages (1 MiB) for the pool, enters the main
kernel at its upper end and supplies the full base/size. The kernel places
two 81920-byte child stacks in its lower part for cooperative workers.

GOP policy
A valid active1920x1080 RGB/BGR mode is preserved. Otherwise up to256 modes
are queried for FullHD. Compatible candidates have widths640..1920 and
heights400..1080. Lower-mode fallback preference is800x600, then640x480,
then the first other compatible candidate. The scan continues after finding
FullHD so a fallback remains available if the preferred SetMode fails.
FullHD requires at least8294400 framebuffer bytes at a1920-pixel stride.
If setting the preferred mode fails, the remembered fallback is attempted;
the firmware's actual current mode is then validated before use.
Every allocated QueryMode Info buffer is released, even for an unsuitable
mode; a FreePool failure stops additional queries.

After SetMode is attempted, the actual current mode is read again and
validated. Stale queried pointers never become the kernel framebuffer.
The fallback current mode may be up to 8192 by 8192 pixels, with stride at
most 16384 pixels, provided width >= 640 and height >= 400. Stride must cover
the visible width, framebuffer size must cover stride*height*4, and address
arithmetic must not wrap. Bit-mask and BltOnly formats are unsupported.
For IA32, the entire claimed framebuffer range must also fit below or end
at the 4 GiB boundary. A high address is never silently truncated.
A missing/unsupported GOP leaves display kind 0 for serial use.

COM1 and exit discipline
COM1 is optional and mirrors loader diagnostics when present. Absence or
transmit timeout disables serial diagnostics instead of halting a valid boot.
File paths and firmware operations do not depend on serial availability.
Actual boot errors still stop. After the first ExitBootServices attempt,
only GetMemoryMap and ExitBootServices use firmware; failure diagnostics use
direct UART operations, not firmware console/protocol methods.

Historical 0.4.0 verification
Focused AMD64 OVMF loader probes validated framebuffer metadata/writes with
COM1 present and absent, plus a serial-only headless fallback. An actual
IA32 OVMF boot completed file loading, ExitBootServices, shared-kernel entry,
GOP display, PS/2 input and disk I/O. Final 0.4.0 ISO integration exercised
both UEFI architectures alongside both BIOS paths and shared-disk persistence.
See verification.ztext for exact release artifacts and captured evidence.
Native VirtualBox execution and physical hardware remain separate unverified
targets; VirtualBox firmware under QEMU is not a native VM test.
